                <pb facs="0311" n="191" sameAs="frus:frus1932v01/pg191"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A4/1134: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Acting Chairman of the American Delegation (Gibson)</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-06-19T12:00:00-04:00">June 19,
                            1932—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>138. Referring to my 136, June 18, 11 p.m. paragraph headed <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Aviation</hi>, first sentence which now reads “All
                    military aviation except observation planes at sea to be abolished” should be
                    changed to read “All military aviation except for intelligence and scouting
                    purposes to be abolished”. This is a variation from instructions previously sent
                    you but is made because the President feels that sentiment of the country
                    demands the retention of military aviation for these purposes even on land.
                    Please note correction.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
